1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,288
This is ECE 358, video 8 a. The topic is reliable data transfer with stop-and-wait. It covers Chapter 3, slides 25 to 31.

2
00:00:12,738 --> 00:00:29,826
After this video, you can name the four problems of a channel and the four mechanisms that fix them. You can draw the stop-and-wait time diagrams. And you can calculate the sender utilization, the throughput, and the efficiency, as in problem set 3.

3
00:00:30,276 --> 00:00:37,679
Start with the problem. Packets travel over an unreliable channel. The channel can do four bad things.

4
00:00:38,129 --> 00:00:42,609
First, it can corrupt a packet. A bit flips. This is a bit error.

5
00:00:43,059 --> 00:00:48,989
Second, it can deliver packets out of order. Here packet 2 arrives before packet 1.

6
00:00:49,439 --> 00:00:53,258
Third, it can lose a packet. Packet 3 never arrives.

7
00:00:53,708 --> 00:01:00,684
Fourth, it can deliver the same packet more than once. Packet 4 arrives twice. This is a duplicate.

8
00:01:01,134 --> 00:01:08,707
The slide gives a mnemonic: BOLD. B for bit errors, O for out of order, L for loss, D for duplicates.

9
00:01:09,157 --> 00:01:20,400
How do we get reliable data transfer over this channel? First, the sender needs feedback. The receiver sends an acknowledgement, an ACK, for a packet that it received.

10
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:32,285
Sequence numbers solve two problems: out-of-order packets and duplicate packets. With a number in each packet, the receiver can put packets in order and find copies.

11
00:01:32,735 --> 00:01:40,713
Timers solve the problem of lost packets. If no ACK comes before the timer expires, the sender retransmits.

12
00:01:41,163 --> 00:01:48,502
Protocols that retransmit in this way are called automatic repeat request protocols, or ARQ.

13
00:01:48,952 --> 00:01:58,573
Error detection codes, such as a checksum, detect bit errors. The receiver discards a bad packet. In this course, this part is implicit.

14
00:01:59,023 --> 00:02:08,986
The simplest ARQ protocol is stop-and-wait. Its main idea has three parts. Send a packet. Start a timer. Wait for an ACK.

15
00:02:09,436 --> 00:02:14,641
If the timer expires before the ACK comes, the sender retransmits the packet.

16
00:02:15,091 --> 00:02:17,011
The sender sends packet 0.

17
00:02:17,461 --> 00:02:20,064
At the same time, it starts its timer.

18
00:02:20,514 --> 00:02:24,141
The receiver gets packet 0 and sends ack 0.

19
00:02:24,591 --> 00:02:27,833
The sender gets ack 0, so it stops the timer.

20
00:02:28,283 --> 00:02:30,545
Now the sender sends packet 1.

21
00:02:30,995 --> 00:02:34,301
The receiver gets packet 1 and sends ack 1.

22
00:02:34,751 --> 00:02:40,191
The sender gets ack 1. It sends the next packet with sequence number 0 again.

23
00:02:40,641 --> 00:02:44,417
The receiver gets this packet 0 and sends ack 0.

24
00:02:44,867 --> 00:02:52,931
Only one packet is in flight at a time. So one bit is enough for the sequence number: 0, 1, 0, 1.

25
00:02:53,381 --> 00:02:58,309
This is why stop-and-wait is also called the alternating bit protocol.

26
00:02:58,759 --> 00:03:04,669
Case b: a packet is lost. First, packet 0 and ack 0 arrive normally.

27
00:03:05,119 --> 00:03:08,575
Then the sender gets ack 0 and sends packet 1.

28
00:03:09,025 --> 00:03:11,158
The channel loses packet 1.

29
00:03:11,608 --> 00:03:15,320
The receiver does not get packet 1. So it sends nothing.

30
00:03:15,770 --> 00:03:21,786
The sender's timer runs, and no ACK comes. The timer expires. This is a timeout.

31
00:03:22,236 --> 00:03:24,220
The sender resends packet 1.

32
00:03:24,670 --> 00:03:28,681
This time packet 1 arrives. The receiver sends ack 1.

33
00:03:29,131 --> 00:03:32,395
The sender gets ack 1 and sends packet 0.

34
00:03:32,845 --> 00:03:36,471
The receiver gets packet 0 and sends ack 0.

35
00:03:36,921 --> 00:03:42,041
This case uses three mechanisms: the ACK, the sequence number, and the timer.

36
00:03:42,491 --> 00:03:44,433
Here, the timer found the loss.

37
00:03:44,883 --> 00:03:49,981
Case c: an ACK is lost. Packet 0 and ack 0 arrive normally.

38
00:03:50,431 --> 00:03:53,738
The sender gets ack 0 and sends packet 1.

39
00:03:54,188 --> 00:03:57,751
Packet 1 arrives. The receiver sends ack 1.

40
00:03:58,201 --> 00:04:00,227
The channel loses ack 1.

41
00:04:00,677 --> 00:04:04,261
The sender does not get an ACK. Its timer expires.

42
00:04:04,711 --> 00:04:06,695
The sender resends packet 1.

43
00:04:07,145 --> 00:04:12,991
The receiver gets packet 1 again. The sequence number tells it that this is a duplicate.

44
00:04:13,441 --> 00:04:18,966
So it discards the packet and does not give it to the app again. It sends ack 1 again.

45
00:04:19,416 --> 00:04:23,256
The sender gets ack 1 and continues with packet 0.

46
00:04:23,706 --> 00:04:32,410
This case also uses the ACK, the sequence number and the timer. Here, the sequence number prevents a duplicate delivery.

47
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:38,364
Case d: a premature timeout. Packet 0 and ack 0 arrive normally.

48
00:04:38,814 --> 00:04:43,465
The sender sends packet 1. The receiver gets it and sends ack 1.

49
00:04:43,915 --> 00:04:45,621
But ack 1 is delayed.

50
00:04:46,071 --> 00:04:50,978
The timer expires before ack 1 arrives. The timeout is too early.

51
00:04:51,428 --> 00:04:53,412
The sender resends packet 1.

52
00:04:53,862 --> 00:04:57,574
Now ack 1 arrives. The sender sends packet 0.

53
00:04:58,024 --> 00:05:03,037
At the same time, the receiver gets the second packet 1. It is a duplicate.

54
00:05:03,487 --> 00:05:06,965
So the receiver discards it and sends ack 1 again.

55
00:05:07,415 --> 00:05:13,281
The sender gets this second ack 1. It is a duplicate ACK, so the sender ignores it.

56
00:05:13,731 --> 00:05:17,358
The receiver gets packet 0 and sends ack 0.

57
00:05:17,808 --> 00:05:21,115
The sender gets ack 0 and sends packet 1.

58
00:05:21,565 --> 00:05:28,946
The rule of the slide: a duplicate packet at the receiver, or a duplicate ACK at the sender, is discarded.

59
00:05:29,396 --> 00:05:40,916
Stop-and-wait is correct. But is it fast? Here is the example of slide 29. A sender and a receiver use a link with a rate R of 1 gigabit per second.

60
00:05:41,366 --> 00:05:47,681
1 gigabit is 10 to the power 9 bits. So R is 10 to the power 9 bits per second.

61
00:05:48,131 --> 00:05:51,331
The propagation delay is 15 ms.

62
00:05:51,781 --> 00:05:54,639
A packet has L equal to 8000 bits.

63
00:05:55,089 --> 00:05:59,399
The time to transmit the packet into the channel is L over R.

64
00:05:59,849 --> 00:06:09,726
That is 8000 bits divided by 10 to the power 9 bits per second. The result is 8 times 10 to the power minus 6 seconds.

65
00:06:10,176 --> 00:06:17,109
10 to the power minus 6 seconds is 1 microsecond. So d trans is 8 microseconds.

66
00:06:17,559 --> 00:06:26,199
1 ms is 1000 microseconds. So 8 microseconds is 0 point 008 ms.

67
00:06:26,649 --> 00:06:35,993
To measure the performance, the slides use the sender utilization, U sender. It is the fraction of time that the sender is busy sending.

68
00:06:36,443 --> 00:06:44,550
Here is the time diagram of slide 30. It is not to scale. The sender transmits the first bit at time 0.

69
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,264
It transmits the last bit at time L over R.

70
00:06:48,714 --> 00:06:54,047
The bits travel for the propagation delay. The first bit arrives after d prop.

71
00:06:54,497 --> 00:07:00,343
The last bit arrives at L over R plus d prop. Then the receiver sends the ACK.

72
00:07:00,793 --> 00:07:04,227
The slides take the ACK transmission time as zero.

73
00:07:04,677 --> 00:07:11,461
The ACK travels back for another d prop. It arrives at the sender at L over R plus 2 d prop.

74
00:07:11,911 --> 00:07:20,146
RTT is the round-trip time, 2 times d prop. So the ACK arrives at RTT plus L over R.

75
00:07:20,596 --> 00:07:23,497
Only then can the sender send the next packet.

76
00:07:23,947 --> 00:07:30,902
So in each cycle of RTT plus L over R, the sender is busy for only L over R.

77
00:07:31,352 --> 00:07:35,256
For the rest of the cycle, the RTT, the sender is idle.

78
00:07:35,706 --> 00:07:42,938
From the diagram, U sender equals L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R.

79
00:07:43,388 --> 00:07:50,492
The values come from slide 29. L over R is 0 point 008 ms.

80
00:07:50,942 --> 00:07:58,366
RTT is 2 times d prop. That is 2 times 15 ms, which is 30 ms.

81
00:07:58,816 --> 00:08:10,720
Put the values into the formula. U sender is 0 point 008 ms, divided by 30 ms plus 0 point 008 ms.

82
00:08:11,170 --> 00:08:21,346
That is 0 point 008 divided by 30 point 008, which is about 0 point 0 0 0 2 7.

83
00:08:21,796 --> 00:08:26,041
As a percentage, it is 0 point 0 2 7 percent.

84
00:08:26,491 --> 00:08:35,025
The bar shows one cycle of 30 point 008 ms, to scale. The orange part is the wait for the ACK.

85
00:08:35,475 --> 00:08:40,253
The blue busy part, L over R, is too thin to see. Here it is zoomed.

86
00:08:40,703 --> 00:08:55,978
30 point 008 divided by 0 point 008 is 3751. So the busy part is only 1 part in 3751 of the cycle.

87
00:08:56,428 --> 00:09:00,353
The throughput is the utilization times the link rate R.

88
00:09:00,803 --> 00:09:14,329
0 point 0 0 0 2 7 times 10 to the power 9 bits per second is 270 thousand bits per second. That is 270 kilobits per second.

89
00:09:14,779 --> 00:09:27,024
With the exact value of U, it is 266 point 6 kilobits per second, over a 1 gigabit per second link. The protocol limits the performance of the link.

90
00:09:27,474 --> 00:09:33,490
Now the question bank. Here are questions 19 and 20. Pause the video and try them.

91
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:38,975
Question 19. First, the channel can corrupt packets with bit errors.

92
00:09:39,425 --> 00:09:41,729
It can deliver packets out of order.

93
00:09:42,179 --> 00:09:43,821
It can lose packets.

94
00:09:44,271 --> 00:09:49,199
And it can deliver duplicates. The four problems are B, O, L, D.

95
00:09:49,649 --> 00:09:56,519
Question 20. The first mechanism is error detection, such as a checksum. It finds bit errors.

96
00:09:56,969 --> 00:10:02,643
The second is the acknowledgement, the ACK, from the receiver. It gives feedback to the sender.

97
00:10:03,093 --> 00:10:08,299
The third is the sequence number. It fixes out-of-order and duplicate packets.

98
00:10:08,749 --> 00:10:13,485
The fourth is the timer. It finds a loss, and the sender retransmits.

99
00:10:13,935 --> 00:10:17,199
Question 21. Pause the video and try it.

100
00:10:17,649 --> 00:10:24,881
Here is a good answer: draw the packet loss case. The sender sends a packet, starts a timer, and waits for the ACK.

101
00:10:25,331 --> 00:10:35,016
Mechanism 1 is the sequence number. The packets carry 0, 1, 0, and so on. The receiver uses it to find duplicates.

102
00:10:35,466 --> 00:10:43,935
Mechanism 2 is the ACK. Ack 0 and ack 1 tell the sender that the packet arrived. Then it sends the next packet.

103
00:10:44,385 --> 00:10:53,303
Mechanism 3 is the timer. Packet 1 is lost, so no ACK comes. At the timeout, the sender retransmits packet 1.

104
00:10:53,753 --> 00:11:04,035
Mechanism 4 is the checksum. The receiver checks each packet. It discards a packet with bit errors, so the sender's timer handles it like a loss.

105
00:11:04,485 --> 00:11:09,733
Questions 22, 23 and 24. Pause the video and try them.

106
00:11:10,183 --> 00:11:14,834
Question 22. ARQ means automatic repeat request.

107
00:11:15,284 --> 00:11:23,561
The sender retransmits a packet automatically when no ACK arrives before the timeout. The receiver does not need to ask.

108
00:11:24,011 --> 00:11:32,118
Question 23. The main drawback is low sender utilization. The sender is idle while it waits for each ACK.

109
00:11:32,568 --> 00:11:47,651
In the example of slide 31, U sender is 0 point 008 divided by 30 point 008. The sender is busy only 0 point 0 2 7 percent of the time.

110
00:11:48,101 --> 00:11:57,551
Question 24. One packet. Stop-and-wait has only one unACKed packet, so a timeout resends only that packet.

111
00:11:58,001 --> 00:12:02,375
Questions 25 and 26. Pause the video and try them.

112
00:12:02,825 --> 00:12:12,062
Question 25. The receiver sends a duplicate ACK when a duplicate packet arrives. It discards the copy and sends the ACK again.

113
00:12:12,512 --> 00:12:16,267
This happens after an ACK loss, as in this diagram.

114
00:12:16,717 --> 00:12:21,645
It also happens after a premature timeout, when the ACK is only delayed.

115
00:12:22,095 --> 00:12:30,436
Question 26. The metric is the sender utilization, U sender: the fraction of time the sender is busy sending.

116
00:12:30,886 --> 00:12:32,955
The throughput is U times R.

117
00:12:33,405 --> 00:12:41,384
Now problem set 3, problem 1. Stop-and-go is another name for stop-and-wait. Pause the video and try it.

118
00:12:41,834 --> 00:12:48,149
Use the symbols of the solution. d is the time to transmit a data frame: L over C.

119
00:12:48,599 --> 00:12:56,535
The data frame is 840 bytes. 840 times 8 is 6720 bits.

120
00:12:56,985 --> 00:13:01,422
P is the propagation delay, 8 ms in each direction.

121
00:13:01,872 --> 00:13:04,368
a is the time to transmit the ACK frame.

122
00:13:04,818 --> 00:13:09,725
The ACK is 40 bytes. 40 times 8 is 320 bits.

123
00:13:10,175 --> 00:13:12,372
The ACK also travels for P.

124
00:13:12,822 --> 00:13:16,811
So one cycle takes T equals d plus 2 P plus a.

125
00:13:17,261 --> 00:13:20,397
The throughput is 1 over T packets per second.

126
00:13:20,847 --> 00:13:27,823
The efficiency is d over T. It is U sender, but this time the ACK transmission time is included.

127
00:13:28,273 --> 00:13:41,927
At 1 kilobit per second, C is 10 to the power 3 bits per second. d is 6720 divided by 10 to the power 3, which is 6 point 7 2 seconds.

128
00:13:42,377 --> 00:13:49,097
a is 320 divided by 10 to the power 3, which is 0 point 3 2 seconds.

129
00:13:49,547 --> 00:14:05,845
2 P is 16 ms, which is 0 point 0 1 6 seconds. So T is 6 point 7 2 plus 0 point 0 1 6 plus 0 point 3 2, which is 7 point 0 5 6 seconds.

130
00:14:06,295 --> 00:14:15,085
The throughput is 1 divided by 7 point 0 5 6 seconds, which is 0 point 1 4 2 packets per second.

131
00:14:15,535 --> 00:14:24,431
The efficiency is 6 point 7 2 divided by 7 point 0 5 6, which is 95 point 2 percent.

132
00:14:24,881 --> 00:14:43,227
At 100 kilobits per second, C is 10 to the power 5 bits per second. d is 6720 divided by 10 to the power 5, which is 0 point 0 6 7 2 seconds, or 67 point 2 ms.

133
00:14:43,677 --> 00:14:50,269
a is 320 divided by 10 to the power 5, which is 3 point 2 ms.

134
00:14:50,719 --> 00:14:58,314
T is 67 point 2 plus 16 plus 3 point 2, which is 86 point 4 ms.

135
00:14:58,764 --> 00:15:07,084
The throughput is 1 divided by 0 point 0 8 6 4 seconds, which is 11 point 6 packets per second.

136
00:15:07,534 --> 00:15:15,726
The efficiency is 67 point 2 divided by 86 point 4, which is 77 point 8 percent.

137
00:15:16,176 --> 00:15:25,179
At 10 megabits per second, C is 10 to the power 7 bits per second. d is 0 point 6 7 2 ms.

138
00:15:25,629 --> 00:15:28,679
a is 0 point 0 3 2 ms.

139
00:15:29,129 --> 00:15:39,199
T is 0 point 6 7 2 plus 16 plus 0 point 0 3 2, which is 16 point 7 0 4 ms.

140
00:15:39,649 --> 00:15:49,441
The throughput is 1 divided by 0 point 0 1 6 7 0 4 seconds, which is 59 point 9 packets per second.

141
00:15:49,891 --> 00:15:59,576
The efficiency is 0 point 6 7 2 divided by 16 point 7 0 4, which is 4 point 0 2 percent.

142
00:16:00,026 --> 00:16:13,189
At 1 gigabit per second, C is 10 to the power 9 bits per second. d is 6 point 7 2 microseconds, which is 0 point 0 0 6 7 2 ms.

143
00:16:13,639 --> 00:16:20,935
a is 0 point 3 2 microseconds, which is 0 point 0 0 0 3 2 ms.

144
00:16:21,385 --> 00:16:34,206
T is 0 point 0 0 6 7 2 plus 16 plus 0 point 0 0 0 3 2, which is about 16 point 0 0 7 ms.

145
00:16:34,656 --> 00:16:44,533
The throughput is 1 divided by 0 point 0 1 6 0 0 7 seconds, which is 62 point 5 packets per second.

146
00:16:44,983 --> 00:16:56,226
The efficiency is 0 point 0 0 6 7 2 divided by 16 point 0 0 7, which is 0 point 0 4 2 percent.

147
00:16:56,676 --> 00:17:03,183
The bars show T split into d, 2 P and a. Blue is d, gray is 2 P, and green is a.

148
00:17:03,633 --> 00:17:14,833
At a high rate, d becomes very small. But the two propagation delays, 2 P equal to 16 ms, do not change. So the efficiency falls.

149
00:17:15,283 --> 00:17:25,949
The throughput cannot go above 1 over 2 P. 1 divided by 0 point 0 1 6 seconds is 62 point 5 packets per second.

150
00:17:26,399 --> 00:17:34,357
Problem 2. ABP is the alternating bit protocol, so stop-and-wait again. Pause the video and try it.

151
00:17:34,807 --> 00:17:43,767
The propagation delay P is the length divided by the speed. 5 km is 5 times 10 to the power 3 meters.

152
00:17:44,217 --> 00:17:57,187
5 times 10 to the power 3, divided by 2 times 10 to the power 8, is 2 point 5 times 10 to the power minus 5 seconds. That is 25 microseconds.

153
00:17:57,637 --> 00:18:06,789
The packet is 1500 bits. The rate is 10 Mbps, which is 10 to the power 7 bits per second.

154
00:18:07,239 --> 00:18:16,285
So d is 1500 divided by 10 to the power 7 seconds, which is 150 microseconds.

155
00:18:16,735 --> 00:18:23,647
The ACK is also 1500 bits. So a is also 150 microseconds.

156
00:18:24,097 --> 00:18:28,342
This diagram is to scale: d and a are each 6 times P.

157
00:18:28,792 --> 00:18:39,907
T is d plus 2 P plus a. That is 150 plus 2 times 25 plus 150, which is 350 microseconds.

158
00:18:40,357 --> 00:18:51,578
The sender transmits one packet per T. 1 divided by 350 microseconds is about 2857 packets per second.

159
00:18:52,028 --> 00:18:55,036
Problem 2 b. Pause the video and try it.

160
00:18:55,486 --> 00:19:02,078
Five identical 5-km links are connected in series. There is a buffer at each connection.

161
00:19:02,528 --> 00:19:06,197
Each link runs its own alternating bit protocol.

162
00:19:06,647 --> 00:19:15,223
When a packet arrives at a buffer, it can go on at once on the next link. So every link carries one packet per T at the same time.

163
00:19:15,673 --> 00:19:27,428
End to end, the rate is still 1 over T. 1 divided by 350 microseconds is about 2857 packets per second.

164
00:19:27,878 --> 00:19:39,739
Now a packet or its ACK is corrupted with probability p on each link. The solution assumes that each try takes T and succeeds with probability 1 minus p.

165
00:19:40,189 --> 00:19:42,984
So the rate is 1 minus p, over T.

166
00:19:43,434 --> 00:20:00,757
For p equal to 0 point 0 1, that is 0 point 9 9 divided by 350 microseconds, about 2829 packets per second. The solution writes about 2828.

167
00:20:01,207 --> 00:20:16,204
Compare one 25-kilometer link. P is 25 times 10 to the power 3 meters, divided by 2 times 10 to the power 8 meters per second. That is 125 microseconds.

168
00:20:16,654 --> 00:20:28,707
T is 150 plus 2 times 125 plus 150, which is 550 microseconds. The bars show both cycles to scale.

169
00:20:29,157 --> 00:20:37,669
The rate is 1 divided by 550 microseconds, about 1818 packets per second.

170
00:20:38,119 --> 00:20:53,074
So 5 short links in series give a higher rate than one long link. On a short link, the sender waits only 2 P, 50 microseconds, for each ACK. On the long link, it waits 250 microseconds.

171
00:20:53,524 --> 00:21:05,940
Here is the recap. One. The channel can cause bit errors, out-of-order delivery, loss, and duplicates. The mechanisms are the checksum, the ACK, the sequence number, and the timer.

172
00:21:06,390 --> 00:21:17,590
Two. Stop-and-wait sends one packet, starts a timer, and waits for the ACK. On a timeout, it resends. The sequence number alternates between 0 and 1.

173
00:21:18,040 --> 00:21:29,453
Three. A duplicate packet comes after an ACK loss or a premature timeout. The receiver discards it and sends the ACK again. The sender ignores a duplicate ACK.

174
00:21:29,903 --> 00:21:47,653
Four. U sender is L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R. In the slide example, it is 0 point 0 2 7 percent, and the throughput is 270 kilobits per second over a 1 gigabit per second link.

175
00:21:48,103 --> 00:22:03,697
Five. In the problem set form, T is d plus 2 P plus a. The throughput is 1 over T. The efficiency is d over T. The next video covers pipelining and Go-Back-N. That is the end of video 8 a.
